Linux runtime performance lab. You have a quiet container: use it to MEASURE the real Linux driver (static/ergopti_plus/linux, LuaJIT) and then optimise the dominant costs. Read .agents/skills/perf-profiling/SKILL.md and .agents/skills/linux-driver/SKILL.md first, and docs/memory/rejected_proposals.md (never re-raise a rejected idea without new evidence).
1. Set up a headless desktop: Xvfb, a window manager (openbox), dbus, and the tools the driver uses (xdotool, wmctrl, xclip, libxcb, sqlite3, lua-luv, lua-filesystem). Real evdev/uinput is probably not available in the container: use the driver's own virtual/stubbed input harnesses (see the "virtual-keyboard E2E harness" in .github/workflows/ci-linux.yml and linux/tests/e2e) and its dry-run modes.
2. Measure, with the driver's permanent counters (commit 9df1e39d: idle loop on input descriptors; boot profiler; logger evidence records) plus `perf stat`/`/proc/<pid>/stat`/`strace -c -f`/LuaJIT `-jp` (profiler) and `-jv` (trace aborts and NYI in the hot path): boot to ready, idle CPU over 120 s (handover claims 99.99% of a core down to 1.5%: validate), RSS after boot and after 10 000 synthetic keystrokes, per-keystroke CPU on the typing path (hotstring matching, repeat key, tap-hold, predictions off/on with a fake LLM), menu/tray build, TOML parse, config reload, hotstring load, X11 focus lookups (libxcb path, commit a8bea52d). Repeat each measurement at least 5 times, report median and spread.
3. Write the results as docs/audits/performance/2026-10-24-linux-runtime-profile.md (measured vs deduced labelled, commands to reproduce).
4. Optimise the dominant costs you measured, one lever per commit, each with before/after numbers in the body and a regression guard (behavioural tests unchanged and green; a counter- or fork-count test where it makes sense). Stay OFF the three levers another agent handles (lazy module requires at boot, the `find` forks of the hotstring loader, the log-rotation forks of logger_sink.lua). LuaJIT trace aborts in the per-key path (NYI functions, string.format/gsub patterns, closures created per key, table churn) are prime targets.
5. Run the full Linux suite before each push.
